1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,930
Every so often, the same headline appears.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:07,710
A plane took off, something went wrong,
and it had to come straight back.

3
00:00:08,541 --> 00:00:10,481
But first, it dumped
twenty tonnes of fuel.

4
00:00:10,561 --> 00:00:15,951
Twenty thousand kilograms of kerosene,
released deliberately into the sky.

5
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:18,278
Which leaves two questions.

6
00:00:19,168 --> 00:00:20,518
Where does it actually go?

7
00:00:21,228 --> 00:00:24,358
And if the problem is too much
fuel, why not simply burn it?

8
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:29,930
Picture it. A large airliner
has just lifted off.

9
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:33,270
A few minutes into the
climb, something goes wrong.

10
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:38,823
An engine problem. A gear that will
not retract. The crew turns back.

11
00:00:38,903 --> 00:00:41,433
Which sounds simple. Turn around, land.

12
00:00:42,296 --> 00:00:46,706
Except the aircraft took off eight minutes
ago, so the tanks are still almost full.

13
00:00:47,516 --> 00:00:49,746
And here the numbers
stop being comfortable.

14
00:00:50,607 --> 00:00:54,887
A seven four seven may take off at
three hundred and ninety-seven tonnes.

15
00:00:54,967 --> 00:00:56,167
It may land at two hundred and eighty-six.

16
00:00:58,191 --> 00:01:00,531
That gap is over a hundred tonnes.

17
00:01:00,851 --> 00:01:05,641
A hundred tonnes of aircraft that is legal
in the air, and not legal on the runway.

18
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:08,797
On a normal flight this never comes up.

19
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:13,437
The aircraft burns fuel for ten
hours and gets lighter on its own.

20
00:01:14,153 --> 00:01:18,243
But turn back fifteen minutes after
departure, and none of that has happened.

21
00:01:19,053 --> 00:01:21,923
It is still carrying nearly
everything it left with.

22
00:01:24,035 --> 00:01:26,005
Which invites an obvious objection.

23
00:01:27,035 --> 00:01:28,135
Why not build the thing stronger?

24
00:01:28,215 --> 00:01:31,245
Heavier undercarriage, stiffer wings.

25
00:01:32,015 --> 00:01:33,565
Then it could land at any weight.

26
00:01:34,419 --> 00:01:38,509
Engineers could. But a stronger
structure means more material.

27
00:01:38,859 --> 00:01:41,209
More material means more mass.

28
00:01:41,499 --> 00:01:44,249
More mass means more fuel to carry it.

29
00:01:44,911 --> 00:01:48,131
And every kilogram of it flies
on every flight the aircraft ever

30
00:01:48,211 --> 00:01:52,521
makes, for decades, to be useful
on the rare day it turns back.

31
00:01:53,284 --> 00:01:55,634
Which does not mean it cannot land heavy.

32
00:01:56,444 --> 00:01:59,254
An overweight landing is
a certified procedure.

33
00:01:59,504 --> 00:02:02,494
If the aircraft needs to
be down now, it lands.

34
00:02:04,832 --> 00:02:08,642
But if there is time, it is better
to arrive at a sensible weight.

35
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:12,622
And the heaviest thing an
aircraft can throw away is fuel.

36
00:02:13,442 --> 00:02:16,452
So where do twenty thousand
kilograms of kerosene go?

37
00:02:17,142 --> 00:02:20,172
Not, as it turns out,
into anybody's garden.

38
00:02:20,985 --> 00:02:24,455
Look at where the fuel leaves.
Not a hatch in the belly.

39
00:02:24,685 --> 00:02:26,295
Not a pipe near the engines.

40
00:02:27,365 --> 00:02:29,255
The nozzles sit out near the wingtips.

41
00:02:30,111 --> 00:02:34,441
As far from everything hot as the
airframe allows. Remember that.

42
00:02:34,791 --> 00:02:36,521
It becomes important later.

43
00:02:37,335 --> 00:02:38,765
And they are not simply holes.

44
00:02:39,715 --> 00:02:41,975
They atomise, spraying
fuel into a slipstream

45
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:46,465
moving at hundreds of kilometres
an hour. What leaves is a mist.

46
00:02:47,312 --> 00:02:48,562
Then there is altitude.

47
00:02:49,552 --> 00:02:54,287
Fuel is normally jettisoned no lower
than about eighteen hundred metres.

48
00:02:54,572 --> 00:02:56,302
And that number is not arbitrary.

49
00:02:57,769 --> 00:03:00,949
Modelling by the American
toxic substances agency found

50
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:04,069
that above roughly fifteen
hundred metres, more than

51
00:03:04,149 --> 00:03:07,479
ninety-eight percent evaporates
before reaching the ground.

52
00:03:08,287 --> 00:03:11,477
Ground sampling under real
dumps found no liquid fuel.

53
00:03:12,387 --> 00:03:13,817
The kerosene does not vanish.

54
00:03:14,607 --> 00:03:15,907
It stays in the atmosphere.

55
00:03:15,987 --> 00:03:18,437
But it does not fall as rain.

56
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:23,960
Which handles the first
question. Now the second.

57
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:28,680
The aircraft has four engines whose
entire purpose is destroying fuel.

58
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:30,940
So why not just burn it?

59
00:03:31,649 --> 00:03:34,939
Because engines burn fuel at
the rate engines burn fuel.

60
00:03:35,629 --> 00:03:40,039
A seven four seven in cruise gets
through ten to eleven tonnes an hour.

61
00:03:40,728 --> 00:03:44,278
So to burn off a hundred tonnes
you would fly for about ten hours.

62
00:03:45,228 --> 00:03:48,918
And you cannot instruct four jet
engines to eat five times faster.

63
00:03:51,207 --> 00:03:53,997
Which is where I had what
felt like a very good idea.

64
00:03:54,807 --> 00:03:56,817
We already spray fuel out of the wings.

65
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:58,957
So set fire to it.

66
00:03:59,649 --> 00:04:03,389
Fuel streaming from the wingtips, and
behind the aircraft, an enormous flame.

67
00:04:03,469 --> 00:04:07,599
Or, to use the technical
term, a flying flamethrower.

68
00:04:08,342 --> 00:04:10,172
So why does nobody do this?

69
00:04:10,902 --> 00:04:13,892
Here is the part I did
not expect. Somebody did.

70
00:04:14,691 --> 00:04:18,151
The F one eleven was a
swing-wing bomber, and its fuel

71
00:04:18,231 --> 00:04:20,901
dump outlet happened to sit
between the two engines.

72
00:04:21,891 --> 00:04:23,731
Which meant that if
you dumped fuel and lit

73
00:04:23,811 --> 00:04:26,501
the afterburners at
once, the fuel ignited.

74
00:04:27,571 --> 00:04:29,521
A flame dozens of metres long.

75
00:04:30,287 --> 00:04:34,217
The Australian air force turned it
into a signature airshow display.

76
00:04:34,967 --> 00:04:37,157
It had no operational purpose.

77
00:04:37,847 --> 00:04:40,057
It was never a weight reduction system.

78
00:04:40,777 --> 00:04:43,347
It was an accident of
where somebody put a pipe.

79
00:04:44,057 --> 00:04:45,947
And nobody else ever took it up.

80
00:04:46,656 --> 00:04:50,886
That flame existed because a fuel
outlet sat next to an afterburner.

81
00:04:51,616 --> 00:04:53,216
On an airliner the nozzles are at the

82
00:04:53,296 --> 00:04:56,166
wingtips, deliberately,
so that never happens.

83
00:04:57,037 --> 00:05:01,287
The choice that makes dumping safe is the
same one that makes burning impossible.

84
00:05:02,477 --> 00:05:06,727
And nobody wants to hear: we have
activated the emergency flamethrower.

85
00:05:09,103 --> 00:05:10,713
So the fuel stays unlit.

86
00:05:10,843 --> 00:05:12,693
But we still need the weight gone.

87
00:05:13,383 --> 00:05:15,123
What else is on board? Luggage.

88
00:05:15,203 --> 00:05:18,693
Two hundred passengers,
twenty kilograms each.

89
00:05:19,530 --> 00:05:24,060
Four tonnes. Against a hundred tonne
problem, that is a rounding error.

90
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:28,440
We would open the cargo doors in
flight to solve four percent of it.

91
00:05:30,523 --> 00:05:32,053
So, not the suitcases.

92
00:05:32,263 --> 00:05:36,173
Which leaves one remaining
source of mass. The passengers.

93
00:05:37,243 --> 00:05:40,113
Obviously, we are not going to
throw people out of an aircraft.

94
00:05:41,098 --> 00:05:44,508
This is a civilised
society. We have parachutes.

95
00:05:44,998 --> 00:05:47,538
The computer decides the
aircraft is twelve tonnes

96
00:05:47,618 --> 00:05:51,028
too heavy, and assumes eighty
kilograms per passenger.

97
00:05:51,884 --> 00:05:54,294
A message appears on
the captain's display.

98
00:05:55,144 --> 00:05:58,134
Recommendation: remove one
hundred and fifty passengers.

99
00:05:58,724 --> 00:06:01,694
Selected at random, of
course. For fairness.

100
00:06:02,608 --> 00:06:08,088
Congratulations. You have been enrolled in
the Emergency Weight Reduction Programme.

101
00:06:08,508 --> 00:06:12,918
Good afternoon. I understand most
of you were expecting Frankfurt.

102
00:06:14,036 --> 00:06:15,586
Instead, a parachute course.

103
00:06:15,956 --> 00:06:20,866
It lasts four minutes. This is
a parachute. This is the cord.

104
00:06:21,496 --> 00:06:23,946
If something goes wrong,
use the other parachute.

105
00:06:24,871 --> 00:06:27,481
And then the cabin door opens. Good luck.

106
00:06:30,359 --> 00:06:33,449
So the fuel goes
overboard instead. Except.

107
00:06:34,199 --> 00:06:36,789
And this is the part
almost nobody mentions.

108
00:06:37,619 --> 00:06:40,009
Most airliners cannot dump fuel at all.

109
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:42,370
The seven three seven cannot.

110
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:44,510
The A three twenty cannot.

111
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:47,800
Neither can the seven
five seven, the A two

112
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,630
twenty, or essentially every regional jet.

113
00:06:51,465 --> 00:06:53,835
They have no jettison system whatsoever.

114
00:06:54,745 --> 00:06:56,375
And that is not an oversight.

115
00:06:57,085 --> 00:06:59,315
It is written into the
certification rules.

116
00:07:00,227 --> 00:07:03,907
An aircraft needs a jettison
system only if it cannot meet its

117
00:07:03,987 --> 00:07:08,357
climb requirements after a fifteen
minute flight at maximum weight.

118
00:07:09,133 --> 00:07:12,853
Narrowbodies can. They are light
enough, and their engines have

119
00:07:12,933 --> 00:07:16,103
enough margin, to climb
away safely even when full.

120
00:07:16,713 --> 00:07:18,423
So nobody fits one.

121
00:07:19,132 --> 00:07:22,852
Which means that for the aircraft
most of us actually fly on, the

122
00:07:22,932 --> 00:07:26,662
answer to where the dumped fuel goes
is that there is no dumped fuel.

123
00:07:27,399 --> 00:07:31,719
A seven three seven that turns back early
circles until it has burned enough off.

124
00:07:31,799 --> 00:07:36,189
Or it lands overweight, and
engineers inspect it afterwards.

125
00:07:38,782 --> 00:07:41,312
Fuel dumping is not the normal solution.

126
00:07:42,202 --> 00:07:43,922
It is the option available to the largest

127
00:07:44,002 --> 00:07:47,252
aircraft, where the weight
gap is simply too big.

128
00:07:47,989 --> 00:07:50,759
So the next time that headline
appears, you will know.

129
00:07:51,649 --> 00:07:55,259
The aircraft was too heavy to land,
and big enough to have a way out.

130
00:07:56,045 --> 00:07:59,275
And kerosene, unlike passengers,
does not need a parachute.

131
00:07:59,465 --> 00:08:01,955
Does not need a four minute course.

132
00:08:02,365 --> 00:08:05,175
And does not, on landing,
request a refund.
